sections=[
('1. Executive summary and project scope',[
'Blind License is an educational digital design marketplace implementing 1-out-of-2 Diffie–Hellman based oblivious transfer. The vendor offers North and Form SVG templates. A buyer selects one locally, sends a blinded group element, receives both encrypted templates, and decrypts the selected template inside the browser. The server API never receives the choice bit or a selected-template URL.',
'The deliverable contains a responsive frontend, Flask REST backend, SQLite session store, libsodium Ristretto255 group operations, SHA-256 transcript-bound key derivation, and AES-256-GCM authenticated encryption. It uses library primitives rather than custom elliptic-curve arithmetic or custom ciphers. Browser assets are vendored locally to avoid runtime CDN requests.',
'This is a tested teaching prototype with a semi-honest cryptographic security model. Payment is explicitly simulated; each new session represents a new demo entitlement. The implementation does not provide a real payment gateway, digital rights management, malicious-party security proof, or protection from a compromised browser. One-transfer enforcement is per session, not per person or real purchase.',
'Local verification: eight automated tests passed, and real browser downloads succeeded for both choices. The package is prepared for Docker hosting. GitHub repository: https://github.com/i242135-cell/blind-license-oblivious-transfer . Public deployment must be verified separately; no hosted URL is claimed in this report.'
]),
('2. Requirements and submission mapping',[
'The supplied Project_Overview_and_Deliverables.docx requires a group of exactly three students, an assigned cryptographic core, a functional full-stack application, explicit threat model, diagrams, source control, live hosting, security experiments, technical PDF documentation, and presentation material. The supplied Project_Topic_Guide.docx identifies this work as topic 5, Oblivious Transfer.',
'Cryptographic core: ot.py implements the DH OT composition using libsodium and cryptography. Key lifecycle and assumptions are specified in sections 5–6. Architecture and protocol data flow appear in sections 3–4. The frontend includes a three-step walkthrough, choice-button tooltips, progress messages, and a protocol observatory.',
'Functional system: app.py exposes session, transfer, and health endpoints; static/index.html performs receiver-side cryptography and local downloading. SQLite persists ephemeral sessions across application restarts. test_security.py and test_results.txt contain reproducible security checks. Dockerfile and render.yaml prepare deployment.',
'Submission artifacts: this editable DOCX, a matching-content PDF, a PPTX with speaker notes, complete source, interface screenshot, benchmark data, and README. Repository: https://github.com/i242135-cell/blind-license-oblivious-transfer . Pending external actions: publish the application over HTTPS and put both URLs in the topic selection sheet. Team names, roll numbers, and section are intentionally blank for the students to complete.',
'The official sheet must contain the group’s own entry only. Do not modify another group’s entry. The brief states no GCR submission is required. The in-class demo and required technical question for the next presenting group remain student activities.'
]),
('3. Architecture and trust boundaries',[
'The browser is the receiver trust domain. It holds the choice c and fresh scalar b, validates the sender point, computes B and its own shared point, derives one key using Web Crypto, decrypts only the chosen ciphertext, and creates a local Blob download. Neither b nor c is placed in an API request, persistent browser storage, or a download URL.',
'The Flask service is the vendor trust domain. It holds both plaintext SVG assets and fresh sender scalar a. It sends public point A, accepts blinded point B, computes two sender shared points, pads and encrypts both messages, and returns both in a fixed order. SQLite stores session ID, sender secret, sender public point, creation time, and consumed flag. It stores no choice column.',
'The HTTP boundary carries only public protocol values, session handles, nonces, and ciphertexts. Deploy with HTTPS to authenticate the server and protect delivered JavaScript. A localhost HTTP demo is usable because browsers treat loopback as a secure context. Third-party analytics and per-template remote image loads are absent.',
'Architecture flow: Buyer browser [c,b; local decryption] <—HTTPS—> Vendor API [a,m0,m1; both encryptions] <—SQL transaction—> Session database. Download flow: ciphertext pair → browser AES-GCM → length-prefixed SVG → local Blob. The original source assets are not exposed under the public static directory.',
'Access logs may still expose IP address, time, user-agent, and participation in a purchase session. This is choice privacy, not buyer anonymity. A malicious vendor controlling the downloaded client code can read the choice directly; independent client verification or a separately distributed client is needed to strengthen that boundary.'
]),
('4. Protocol specification and correctness',[
'Let G be a prime-order group with generator P. The implementation uses Ristretto255 in additive notation; this is elliptic-curve Diffie–Hellman. Sender samples a nonzero scalar a and sends A = aP with a fresh 128-bit session identifier. Receiver privately chooses c in {0,1}, samples nonzero b, and sends B = bP + cA.',
'Sender computes S0 = aB and S1 = a(B − A). Receiver computes S = bA. If c = 0, S0 = a(bP) = abP = bA. If c = 1, S1 = a((bP + A) − A) = abP = bA. Thus the receiver agrees with exactly the intended sender branch under the protocol assumptions.',
'For branch i, K_i = SHA256(ASCII("OT-LICENSE-v1") || sid16 || A32 || B32 || byte(i) || shared32). Fixed-width fields and the domain prefix avoid ambiguous encodings and bind the output to this session and branch. Receiver uses the same construction with i = c and shared = bA. This is a hash-based key derivation in a random-oracle-style analysis; it is not an independently proven production OT implementation.',
'Each message is encoded as uint32_be(length) || SVG bytes || zero padding to 4,096 bytes. AES-256-GCM uses a fresh random 96-bit nonce for each branch, authenticates sid16 || byte(i), and emits 4,112 ciphertext bytes including its 16-byte tag. Both branches and both nonces are returned together. The browser verifies GCM, checks the length bound, and downloads the unpadded bytes.',
'Sender and receiver points are validated. Identity A or B is rejected; sender also rejects identity B−A. Degenerate valid samples have negligible probability and the UI can restart. A consumed session is never reopened after a transfer. A network failure after commit may lose a demo entitlement; a production design needs idempotent retransmission of the same stored response.'
]),
('5. Threat model and security reasoning',[
'Assets: the buyer’s choice, the unchosen template, the buyer scalar, the sender scalar, session integrity, and plaintext integrity. Baseline adversaries are a vendor or buyer that follows the OT computation but inspects everything in its own view. Cryptographic assumptions include hardness of the relevant discrete-log/DH problems in the selected group and suitable hashing and authenticated encryption. Endpoints and library binaries are assumed uncompromised.',
'Receiver privacy: conditioned on A, a fresh uniformly sampled b makes B look uniform (up to negligible excluded identity cases) for either c. Adding A shifts the group distribution rather than revealing a label. The server sends the same pair of payloads for both choices, and no subsequent chosen-file request or decryption acknowledgement is sent.',
'Sender privacy intuition: knowing b and A gives bA for one branch. Obtaining the other shared point additionally involves the unavailable aA = a²P term. This explanation is an intuition under DH-related hardness and hashing assumptions, not a proof against every malicious receiver. A failed wrong-branch decryption test demonstrates behavior for a particular receiver strategy and does not establish general sender security.',
'Out of scope: fully malicious OT participants, selective-failure leakage when users report which decryption failed, compromised frontend code, endpoint malware, quantum adversaries, browser extensions, and theft of sender database files. AES-GCM detects alteration but does not force a malicious vendor to provide correct or useful designs.',
'Exactly one means one decryptable branch per honest protocol session, with computational sender privacy under the stated model. It does not mean a user can never buy again, share a downloaded SVG, take a screenshot, or obtain an asset from another source. The demo makes no real-world licensing enforcement claim.'
]),
('6. Key lifecycle and implementation decisions',[
'Generation: sender a and receiver b come from libsodium scalar generation backed by its secure random source. Session IDs and GCM nonces use Python secrets. No Math.random(), timestamps, sequential IDs, or fixed test secrets are used for cryptographic entropy. Each session generates a fresh sender scalar; each browser operation generates a fresh receiver scalar.',
'Distribution: only A and B cross the network; encryption keys are derived independently from group operations. The session identifier is an unguessable bearer handle with a five-minute lifetime. It is not an account or payment authorization token. Production entitlement issuance must be connected to a verified payment event and an atomic unique purchase record.',
'Storage: sender secrets are temporarily stored in SQLite so a multi-request exchange can complete. The receiver scalar remains in browser memory. On successful server response preparation, a SQL transaction marks the session consumed and sets its secret field to NULL. Old rows are deleted during subsequent session creation. Browser sodium.memzero clears b and the shared-point array on the successful path.',
'Revocation: expired or consumed sessions reject transfer. To revoke an outstanding session operationally, delete its record or mark it consumed. There is no persistent public-key certificate or long-term OT private key. HTTPS certificate lifecycle belongs to the hosting service. Revocation cannot retract plaintext already downloaded.',
'Limitations: setting a database cell to NULL is not secure disk erasure; SQLite journals, backups, and freed pages may retain old values. Python and JavaScript runtimes cannot guarantee erasure of all copied secrets, and error paths may retain temporary arrays until garbage collection. Protect the host and database, avoid debug logging, and use hardened native memory handling for a stronger deployment.'
]),
('7. API, state management and reproducibility',[
'POST /api/session accepts an empty JSON object and returns sid, A, expires_in = 300, and an explicit simulated-payment notice. Pending state holds secret, public point, creation timestamp, and used = 0. A global capacity bound of 1,000 recent rows limits growth; this is not a per-client rate limiter.',
'POST /api/transfer accepts exactly the needed values {"sid": "32 hex characters", "B": "64 hex characters"}. It returns items[0] and items[1], each with nonce and ciphertext encoded as hex. It validates inputs and uses BEGIN IMMEDIATE before checking and consuming a session. The transaction serializes competing redemption attempts. Invalid group input returns 400; expired, unknown, or consumed sessions return 409.',
'GET / serves the responsive UI. GET /health returns {"status":"ok"}. Static assets contain locally vendored libsodium JavaScript. Responses carry no-store, nosniff, and no-referrer headers. JSON request size is bounded at 2 KiB. Default Flask handling rejects malformed JSON or unsupported content type.',
'Reproduce on Linux: install libsodium23, create a Python virtual environment, pip install -r requirements.txt, run python -m unittest test_security -v, then python app.py and open http://127.0.0.1:5000. The development server binds only to localhost. Docker provides the system dependency and runs a single Gunicorn worker for hosted use.',
'Important persistence boundary: SQLite is a local single-instance store. A free hosting service may discard local files on restart; outstanding sessions then expire operationally. Do not deploy independent replicas sharing no database and expect session continuity. For scale, use a transactional shared store and preserve the same one-time-consumption invariant.'
]),
('8. Attack experiments and observed results',[
'Experiment 1 — wrong branch: the test obtains S = bA for c = 0, derives a candidate branch-1 key using that same shared value, and attempts to decrypt item 1. Authentication fails as expected. This rules out the naive “reuse selected shared secret for the other branch” attempt; it is not a general cryptanalytic attack.',
'Experiment 2 — ciphertext tampering: flip one ciphertext byte before decryption. AES-GCM rejects it rather than returning modified plaintext. This demonstrates integrity enforcement using a genuine ciphertext. Experiment 3 — replay: send the identical successful transfer request again. First request returns 200 and second returns 409 because the session was consumed.',
'Experiment 4 — invalid group input: submit the all-zero Ristretto identity as B. The server returns 400. Experiment 5 — expiry: force the session timestamp outside the five-minute window; transfer returns 409. Experiment 6 — metadata equality: both branches contain 4,112 ciphertext bytes, reducing direct payload-length distinctions.',
'Correctness checks: both c = 0 and c = 1 recover the expected original SVG. Lifecycle check: the sender secret field is NULL after successful consumption. All eight unittest cases passed locally. test_results.txt preserves the actual run output. Browser automation additionally clicked both template buttons and observed successful local SVG downloads.',
'Attack surface still open: anonymous session flooding up to the capacity bound, global capacity denial of service, lack of real payment authentication, compromised JavaScript, backups containing ephemeral secrets, and malicious-vendor selective failure. Required next mitigations are entitlement integration, per-client quotas, independent client integrity, audited malicious-secure OT, and protected secret storage. No brute-force or timing success rate is fabricated.'
]),
('9. Performance and security–usability trade-offs',[
f'The measured local benchmark used 10 warm-up rounds and 100 recorded rounds on Python {b["python"]}. Each round included an in-process Flask session request, receiver public-point calculation, and server transfer request. Median: {b["median_ms"]} ms; 95th percentile: {b["p95_ms"]} ms; range: {b["min_ms"]}–{b["max_ms"]} ms. These timings exclude browser decryption, TLS, network delay, and cloud startup.',
'Each branch transmits a 12-byte nonce plus a 4,112-byte authenticated ciphertext. The pair therefore carries 8,248 binary bytes before JSON syntax. Hex encoding doubles this to 16,496 characters before property names and punctuation. Two public points add 64 binary bytes across the OT exchange; the session identifier adds 16 bytes.',
'Padding is expensive for these small sample SVGs but keeps both encrypted payloads equal in size. A production catalog could use size buckets and transfer encrypted content keys with OT, followed by equalized asset fetching. Any per-asset fetch endpoint must be designed carefully because a direct selected URL would reveal the choice.',
'Fresh secrets, authenticated encryption, and point validation add modest local cost; actual user latency will often be dominated by network exchanges. A five-minute expiry reduces secret retention but can interrupt slow users. Atomic consume-before-response prevents a second transfer but can deny a retry after network loss. Availability and privacy should be tested together.',
'The roughly 128-bit classical security target of Ristretto255 does not imply 256-bit overall security merely because the encryption key is 256 bits. These numbers are local engineering measurements and protocol design analysis, not cloud service-level guarantees or empirical proof of privacy.'
]),
('10. Deployment, demo and defense preparation',[
'GitHub procedure: create a new repository, commit the source and documents while excluding sessions.sqlite3, caches and secrets, then push. The included Render blueprint uses Docker and a /health check. Connect the repository to a hosting account, deploy one service, and verify both choices at the resulting HTTPS URL before placing that URL in the topic sheet. This report does not invent a repository address or a deployment success.',
'Live demonstration (about six minutes): explain the two vendor templates and the private browser choice; open developer tools Network; select North; inspect requests to show only sid and B; inspect the equal-sized ciphertext pair; open the downloaded SVG. Start a fresh session and repeat for Form. Run the attack tests to demonstrate replay rejection and tamper detection. Explain the simulation notice before any discussion of payment.',
'Fallback: run the Docker image or localhost server before class, retain the screenshot and test log, and keep a copy of both expected SVG designs. A local fallback does not satisfy the public hosting requirement. Test network access, browser download permissions, and the live health endpoint before presenting.',
'Three-person presentation plan (suggested, not an assertion of individual contributions): member 1 explains problem, architecture and UI; member 2 derives the OT equations and threat model; member 3 demonstrates attacks, results and limitations. Fill in actual names and contributions only after your team agrees.',
'Defense prompts: Why does B hide c? Because adding A shifts an almost-uniform group element. Why are both ciphertexts returned? To avoid a revealing selected-file request. Does GCM prove vendor honesty? No, it authenticates a ciphertext under the derived key. Why not X25519 alone? This construction also requires group addition/subtraction. Can users buy twice? Yes; actual entitlement issuance is not implemented. Question for the next group: which trust assumption would most seriously invalidate your guarantee, and what evidence tests that boundary?'
]),
('11. References and implementation limitations',[
'[1] Course materials: Project_Overview_and_Deliverables.docx and Project_Topic_Guide.docx, read from Downloads. These define requirements and the assigned marketplace case.',
'[2] Tung Chou and Claudio Orlandi, The Simplest Protocol for Oblivious Transfer (2015). https://eprint.iacr.org/2015/267 — reference for the DH OT structure. The presented prototype is an educational composition, not the authors’ audited implementation or a claim of their full security theorem.',
'[3] Libsodium official documentation, Ristretto255. https://libsodium.gitbook.io/doc/advanced/point-arithmetic/ristretto — group operations, encoding, scalar generation and validation. Backend calls the system libsodium shared library; browser uses libsodium-wrappers-sumo 0.7.15.',
'[4] Python cryptography official documentation, Authenticated encryption. https://cryptography.io/en/latest/hazmat/primitives/aead/ — AES-GCM API, nonce discipline and authentication failures. Browser uses Web Crypto AES-GCM and SHA-256.',
'Known limitations to state during defense: simulated payments; anonymous unthrottled session issuance within a global cap; local SQLite persistence; no secure erasure guarantee; no independently audited security proof; no malicious-party guarantee; no public deployment verified yet. These are explicit engineering boundaries, not omitted claims of completion.'
])]
